The Irish [[definite article]] has two forms: {{lang|ga|an}} and {{lang|ga|na}}. {{lang|ga|An}} may cause [[lenition]], [[eclipsis]], or neither. {{lang|ga|Na}} may cause eclipsis, but the only instance of lenition with {{lang|ga|na}} is with the genitive singular of the word {{lang|ga|céad}} meaning ''first''. {{lang|ga|An}} is used in the common case singular for all nouns, and lenites feminine nouns. In the genitive singular, {{lang|ga|an}} with lenition is used with masculine nouns, {{lang|ga|na}} with feminine nouns. In the dative singular, {{lang|ga|an}} may cause lenition or eclipsis depending on the preposition preceding it and on regional norms (in Ulster usage, lenition is standard with all prepositions, while in other regions eclipsis is used with many). {{lang|ga|Na}} is the only plural form of the article; it causes eclipsis in the genitive for both genders, and no mutation in other cases.

Names of countries usually take the definite article in the nominative: {{lang|ga|An Fhrainc}} "France", {{lang|ga|an Bhrasaíl}} "Brazil", {{lang|ga|an tSeapáin}} "Japan". Exceptions to this include {{lang|ga|Éire}} "Ireland", {{lang|ga|Albain}} "Scotland" and {{lang|ga|Sasana}} "England".

There is no [[indefinite article]] in Irish; the word appears by itself, for example: {{lang|ga|Tá peann agam.}} – "I have a pen", {{lang|ga|Tá madra sa seomra.}} – "There's a dog in the room".

When two definite noun phrases appear as part of a genitive construction (equivalent to ''the X of the Y'' in English), only the noun phrase in the genitive takes the article. Compare {{lang|ga|Áras '''an''' Uachtaráin}} or {{lang|ga|Teitheamh '''na''' nIarlaí}} to English '''''the''' residence of '''the''' President, '''the''' flight of '''the''' Earls.''

==Adjectives==
{{Main|Irish declension#Adjectives}}

Irish adjectives always follow the noun. The adjective is influenced by the case, number and gender of the noun preceding it.

* {{lang|ga|An cailín beag}} "The small girl" – masculine singular nominative
* {{lang|ga|An bhean bhocht}} "The poor woman" – feminine singular nominative
* {{lang|ga|Na buachaillí óga}} "The young boys" – masculine plural nominative

Adjectives in Irish have two morphological degrees of [[comparison (grammar)|comparison]]: the positive ({{langx|ga|bunchéim}}), e.g. {{lang|ga|Tá an buachaill '''cairdiúil'''}} "the boy is friendly", and the [[comparative]] ({{lang|ga|breischéim}}), e.g. {{lang|ga|Tá an cailín '''níos cairdiúla''' ná an buachaill}} "the girl is nicer than the boy".  A [[superlative]] ({{lang|ga|sárchéim}}) sense is rendered by the comparative in a relative clause, e.g. {{lang|ga|Is é Seán an páiste '''is cairdiúla''' den triúr}} "Seán is the nicest child of the three".

== Adverbs ==
Irish adverbs are used to modify verbs, adjectives and other adverbs.

An adverb can be created from an adjective by adding {{lang|ga|go}} before it, e.g. {{lang|ga|go mall}}, {{lang|ga|go tapaidh}}, {{lang|ga|go maith}}, etc. If the adjective begins with a vowel, {{vr|h}} is added before it, e.g. {{lang|ga|go hálainn}}, {{lang|ga|go híseal}}, {{lang|ga|go háirithe}}, etc.

Adverbs can often be created from nouns by putting a preposition before them, e.g. {{lang|ga|ar bith}}, {{lang|ga|de ghnáth}}, {{lang|ga|faoi dheireadh}}, etc.

Other categories of adverbs include the following:

Adverbs that describe relation to time, e.g. {{lang|ga|uaireanta}}, {{lang|ga|anois}}, {{lang|ga|cheana}}, etc.

Adverbs that describe relation to place, e.g. {{lang|ga|ann}}, {{lang|ga|abhaile}}, {{lang|ga|amuigh}}, etc.

Adverbs used in questions, e.g. {{lang|ga|cathain?}}, {{lang|ga|conas?}}, {{lang|ga|cá?}}, etc.

Adverbs used for negation, e.g. {{lang|ga|ní}}, {{lang|ga|nach}}, {{lang|ga|nár}}, etc.

Other adverbs, e.g. {{lang|ga|áfach}}, {{lang|ga|chomh maith}}, {{lang|ga|ach oiread}}, etc.

==Verbs==
{{Main|Irish conjugation}}

There are two conjugations and 11 irregular verbs. [[grammatical tense|Tenses]] or [[grammatical mood|moods]] are formed by inflecting the [[word stem|stem]], and in the [[past tense|past]] and [[habitual aspect|habitual]] past tenses and the [[conditional mood]] also by leniting any initial consonant. The inflected tense and mood forms are: present indicative, present habitual indicative (differs from present only in the verb {{lang|ga|bí}} "to be"), future, past indicative, past habitual indicative, conditional, imperative, present subjunctive, and past subjunctive. Verbs also have a [[verbal noun]] and past [[participle]], and [[progressive aspect|progressive]] constructions similar to those using the English present participle may be formed from the verbal noun and an appropriate tense of {{lang|ga|bí}}. Examples of tense conjugations: (all third person forms without subject pronoun):

* 1st conjugation: {{lang|ga|Fág}} "to leave" – {{lang|ga|d'fhág}} (past) – {{lang|ga|fágann}} (present) – {{lang|ga|fágfaidh}} (future) – {{lang|ga|d'fhágfadh}} (conditional) – {{lang|ga|d'fhágadh}} (habitual past) – {{lang|ga|fága}} (subjunctive) – {{lang|ga|fágadh}} (imperative)
* 2nd conjugation: {{lang|ga|Ceannaigh}} "to buy" – {{lang|ga|cheannaigh}} (past) – {{lang|ga|ceannaíonn}} (present) – {{lang|ga|ceannóidh}} (future) – {{lang|ga|cheannódh}} (conditional) – {{lang|ga|cheannaíodh}} (habitual past) – {{lang|ga|ceannaí}} (subjunctive) – {{lang|ga|ceannaíodh}} (imperative)
* Irregular: {{lang|ga|Téigh}} "to go" – {{lang|ga|chuaigh}} (past) – {{lang|ga|téann}} (present) – {{lang|ga|rachaidh}} (future) – {{lang|ga|rachadh}} (conditional) – {{lang|ga|théadh}} (habitual past) – {{lang|ga|té}} (subjunctive) – {{lang|ga|téadh}} (imperative)

In addition to the passive voice, there is the [[impersonal verb|impersonal form of the verb]], termed the {{lang|ga|saorbhriathar}} or "autonomous verb", which serves a similar function (the most literal translation is "You/One/They...[e.g. say, are, do]").

Verbs can be conjugated either [[synthetic language|synthetically]] (with the [[personal pronoun]] included in the verb inflection) or [[analytic language|analytically]] (with the verb inflected for tense only and a separate subject). However, the official standard generally prescribes the analytic form in most person-tense combinations, and the synthetic in only some cases, such as the first person plural. The analytic forms are also generally preferred in the western and northern dialects, except in answer to what would in English be "yes/no" questions, while [[Munster Irish]] prefers the synthetic forms. For example, the following are the standard form, synthetic form and analytical form of the past tense of {{lang|ga|rith}} "to run":

