====Conjunctive forms====
The normal [[word order]] in Irish is verb–subject–object ([[Verb–subject–object|VSO]]). The forms of the [[subject (grammar)|subject]] pronoun directly following the verb are called ''conjunctive.''

The form {{Lang|ga|muid}} in the 1st person plural has only recently been approved for use in the official standard, but is very common in western and northern dialects. The standard and southern dialects have no subject pronoun in the 1st person plural, using the synthetic verb ending {{lang|ga|-imíd}} (alternatively {{lang|ga|-imid}}) instead.

Irish has no [[T–V distinction]], i.e. it does not differentiate between formal and familiar forms of second person pronouns. The difference between {{Lang|ga|tú}} and {{Lang|ga|sibh}} is purely one of number.

There is no equivalent to the English "it". Either {{lang|ga|sé}} or {{lang|ga|sí}} are used depending on whether the thing the speaker is referring to is a masculine noun or a feminine noun. The exception is the pronoun {{lang|ga|ea}}, used in impersonal copula phrases, particularly in the phrases {{lang|ga|is ea}} (> {{lang|ga|sea}}) "yes", "so", "that is so", {{lang|ga|ní hea}} (the opposite of {{lang|ga|is ea}}), {{lang|ga|nach ea?}} "is that not so?", {{lang|ga|an ea}} (Kerry {{lang|ga|am b'ea}}) "Is that so?", {{lang|ga|fear is ea é}} "it's a man", and so on.

====Disjunctive forms====
If a pronoun is not the subject or if a subject pronoun does not follow the verb (as in a [[verbless clause]], or as the subject of the copula, where the pronoun stands at the end of the sentence), the so-called [[disjunctive pronoun|''disjunctive'' forms]] are used:

In Munster dialects the form {{lang|ga|thú}} is either (a) archaic (replaced by {{lang|ga|tú}}) or (b) is only found after words ending in a vowel.

;Standard:{{lang|ga|Buailim thú}} ("I hit you", present tense), {{lang|ga|Bhuail mé thú}} ("I hit you", past tense)
;Dialect type (a):{{lang|ga|Buailim tú}}, {{lang|ga|Bhuail mé tú}}
;Dialect type (b):{{lang|ga|Buailim tú}}, {{lang|ga|Bhuail mé thú}}

====Intensive forms====
Irish also has [[intensive pronoun]]s, used to give the pronouns a bit more weight or emphasis.

The word {{Lang|ga|féin}} ({{IPA|/heːnʲ/}} or {{IPA|/fʲeːnʲ/}}) "-self" can follow a pronoun, either to add emphasis or to form a reflexive pronoun.

:{{Lang|ga|Rinne mé féin é.}} "I did it myself."
:{{Lang|ga|Ar ghortaigh tú thú féin?}} "Did you hurt yourself?"
:{{Lang|ga|[[Sinn Féin]]}} is thus "We Ourselves"

===Prepositional pronouns===
As the object of a preposition, a pronoun is fused with the preposition; one speaks here of "inflected" prepositions, or, as they are more commonly termed, [[conjugated preposition|prepositional pronouns]].

